# Commitlint (Conventional Commits)

Every git commit message MUST follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) in commitlint form:

```text
type(scope)!: subject

optional body

optional footer
```

## Format rules

- **type** (required): one of `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`
- **scope** (optional): short noun for the area, e.g. `lib`, `ui`, `api`, `jobs`, `security`, `ci`, `cursor`
- **!** after type/scope marks a breaking change; also add a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer when applicable
- **subject** (required): imperative, lowercase start, no trailing period, ≤72 characters preferred
- Blank line between subject and body when a body is present
- Body explains **why**, not a file list
- Do not wrap the subject in quotes inside the message itself

## Examples

```text
feat(api): add safe public job URL fetch route

Blocks private and non-HTTPS destinations before connecting.
```

```text
fix(ui): stop putting CSP nonce on body

Browsers hide nonce attributes and broke hydration.
```

```text
chore(cursor): add commitlint conventional-commit rule
```

## When committing

- Prefer several small commits by concern over one dump commit
- Never mix unrelated refactors into a `feat` commit
- Use `test` for test-only changes, `ci` for workflows/scripts that gate CI, `docs` for README/PLAN-only updates
- If amending is requested, the resulting message must still satisfy this format
